Problem
-------

vmbus_alloc_ring() allocates both ring halves with one order-7
(512 KiB) alloc_pages(). Under buddy fragmentation that fails with
-ENOMEM while plenty of virtual memory is free. A dynamically
instantiated sub-channel cannot open and the channel is dead for the
life of the fragmentation. On WSL2 this has been observed as
"accept4 failed 110: Connection timed out" on vsock channels; the
allocation failure is measured, the specific symptom is one report.

Why not just vzalloc()
----------------------

Ring memory must be host-visible. A CoCo guest's private memory has
to be explicitly decrypted before the host can read it, and
set_memory_decrypted() operates on the direct map, so it needs
physically contiguous pages. A vmalloc range is not direct-mapped.
arm64 CCA and Intel TDX guests without a paravisor would break.

Carr's vmbus_alloc_buffer() already solves this: it allocates the
buffer as physically contiguous chunks descending from high order to
order 0, decrypts each chunk where the platform requires it, and
vmap()s them into one virtually contiguous range. v2 routes
everything through it and removes the second allocator.

What changed
------------

- Rings and UIO buffers go through vmbus_alloc_buffer(). Address,
  page array and GPADL state are grouped in struct vmbus_buffer
  with struct vmbus_gpadl embedded, so ownership cannot be split
  across two lifetimes.

- vmbus_buffer_pin_pages()/vmbus_buffer_unpin_pages() close the
  mmap-versus-reclaimer race. The pin record is filled entirely
  under vmbus_buffer_owners_lock, so a pin taken during teardown
  snapshots the protection state the teardown is still holding.
  Uncertain cleanup preserves chunks rather than freeing them;
  unproven GPADL or encryption state is retained and flagged.

- The sysfs "ring" mmap path installs no vm_ops.
  kernfs_fop_mmap() rejects any vma->vm_ops that carries .close
  and reads its state from file->private_data, never from
  vma->vm_private_data, so the pin cannot ride the vma the way the
  /dev/uioN path does. The synchronous wrapper owns that pin and
  releases it after __compat_vma_mmap() on every outcome. The UIO
  path keeps { .mapped, .close }; that path is not kernfs.

- GPADL post-failure injection and order-descent fallback are
  factored for KUnit. The same helpers run in production.

- The five exported symbols keep their prototypes and encryption
  contracts. New _owned entry points and vmbus_release_buffer()
  are EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL for migrated in-tree callers.

- The requestor array uses kvcalloc() (0011) and rndis_request
  uses kvzalloc_obj() (0012), removing two high-order allocations
  from the subchannel-open path. Both are guest-private.

- vmbus_needs_shared_pages() consults both isolation and
  CC_ATTR_GUEST_MEM_ENCRYPT plus the per-buffer flag.

- Reclaim workqueue teardown cancels timer-pending delayed_work
  before destroy_workqueue(). HV_GPADL_BUFFER_DECRYPTED is gone.

- netvsc_send_completion() no longer dereferences a NULL request
  address on empty completions (0013). Control RNDIS sends
  register NULL; the empty-payload branch was a fatal NULL deref
  in NAPI/softirq. Reachable because open now survives
  fragmentation. Kept here so the drill is green on the posted
  bytes; a split is available if netvsc maintainers prefer.

Fixes tags
----------

0011 fixes a regression from e8b7db38449ac ("Drivers: hv: vmbus:
Add vmbus_requestor data structure for VMBus hardening", Andres
Beltran, 2020-11-09), whose request_arr_init() allocated the
requestor array with kcalloc(size, sizeof(u64), GFP_KERNEL) on the
subchannel-open path. 0012 and 0013 harden against buddy
fragmentation and an empty-completion case whose originating
commits are not pinned down here, so they deliberately carry no
Fixes: tag rather than an invented one. Routing 0011-0013 to the
netvsc maintainers as a follow-up is fine if they prefer that
split; the bytes are independent of 0001-0010.

Patch granularity is deliberate. 10/13 is the largest at 1283
changed lines across seven files (1275 insertions, 8 deletions) and
it adds twelve KUnit cases. It is one patch because it is one
contract: the pin record and its lock, the kernfs-safe mmap path,
the synchronous wrapper that owns the pin, and the named cases that
prove each release path are one thing. Splitting it produces a
revision where a pin outlives the lock that proved its protection
state, or where the sysfs path installs vm_ops kernfs will reject.
8/13 is next at 898 changed lines across six files (778 insertions,
120 deletions) and adds five KUnit cases; it is one lifecycle: the
owner record, the three encryption facts, the reclaim scheduling
and the workqueue teardown are one state machine. Splitting it
would produce intermediate revisions in which a buffer can be freed
with an unproven encryption state, or a module unload can strand an
armed reclaim timer against a destroyed workqueue. The other
eleven patches are 14 to 340 lines each.

Mapping to the 2026-09-22 review
--------------------------------

  1. "Use vmbus_alloc_buffer() for rings, not only as a fallback."
     vmbus_alloc_ring() and vmbus_free_ring() are converted in 0001
     and no second ring allocator remains beside them.

  2. "Group the buffer address, page array and GPADL state."
     struct vmbus_buffer in include/linux/hyperv.h, 0001.

  3. "Fold struct vmbus_gpadl into struct vmbus_buffer."
     channel->ringbuffer_gpadlhandle and channel->ringbuffer_page
     are replaced by channel->ringbuffer, whose GPADL is embedded
     as buffer->gpadl and is released by vmbus_release_buffer() as
     one unit, 0001 and 0008. The struct vmbus_gpadl type survives
     as the argument of the three previously exported
     establish/teardown prototypes; nothing that used to allocate
     it separately still does.

  4. "A leak flag when GPADL or CoCo re-encryption cannot be proven."
     gpadl.leak and buffer.leak are set when a GPADL message may
     have reached the host but completion is uncertain, so the
     backing pages are retained instead of handed back, 0001 and
     0008.

  5. "Eliminate redundant HV_GPADL_* types once vmbus_alloc_buffer()
     is universal." HV_GPADL_BUFFER_DECRYPTED is gone. The
     caller-decrypted case is a memory_prepared argument to the
     establish path rather than a third GPADL type, 0001.

Testing
-------

The qualifying pair for these thirteen bytes is
37173468641 / 37173468629 on fork commit 07c1c8ce4d7f, against
pinned mainline base
93f51579e7df ("Linux 7.3-rc4"), with all thirteen series/SHA256SUMS
entries verified before build.

Build: all thirteen stages applied on x86_64 and arm64. The
per-stage gate is scripts/checkpatch.pl --strict --no-tree over
the cumulative working-tree git diff left by git apply, the shape
CI runs: 0 ERROR / 0 WARNING at every stage, "total: 0 errors, 0
warnings, 0 checks" thirteen times in the run's logs. Diffing
committed trees instead reports 0 ERROR and one WARNING from
stage 2 on -- FILE_PATH_CHANGES, "added, moved or deleted
file(s), does MAINTAINERS need updating?" -- because that shape
carries the new files' "new file mode" line and git apply leaves
them untracked. The 28 named KUnit cases this series adds all
pass on x86_64 (hyperv-vmbus-buffer 16, hyperv-uio-mmap 12); the
run reports "Ran 32 tests: passed: 32" including the pre-existing
refcount_interrupt 4. All four CoCo gate self-test injections
rejected on both arches. WSL backport: "Ran 15 tests: passed: 15"
(hyperv-vmbus-gpadl-lifetime 12, hyperv-uio-hv-generic-mmap 3).

As a mail patch, 2/13 and 10/13 print that same FILE_PATH_CHANGES
nudge once; those are the two patches that add a file. The test
files are covered by the existing Hyper-V and UIO directory
entries. Nothing was reshaped to silence it.

WSL backport builds with W=1 and Sparse. Baseline diagnostics that
pre-exist this series are not introduced by it.

Runtime: both hosted guests booted the same bytes as Gen2
(kernel=7.3.0-rc4+). Both legs printed HYPERV_DRILL_RESULT
status=PARTIAL with lifecycle=3 fragment=0 splats=0 faults=0 and
scope=lifecycle-inconclusive. PHASE1 returned to its exact map
baseline after 100 hv_netvsc bind/unbind cycles (cycle_fails=0, 400
steps) at 12 maps / 20279296 bytes / 4939 pages. The hold-in-mmap
window was open on both surfaces and both were live at
establishment: /dev/uio0 touch_ready=5 and the channel "ring" sysfs
node touch_ready=1, each reaching its own release signal
(MMAP_HOLD release_wait on both; waited_ms 100-200 of 60000). The
UIO hold then still read on every map after teardown (touch ok=5 on
maps=5, and MMAP_HOLD released maps=5). The sysfs hold's post-hold
read took the helper (status=failed), which is that surface's own
lifetime rather than a defect: the mapping is withdrawn at node
removal.
high_order_7plus driven to zero with exhausted=1, order7_dmesg=0
accept4_failures=0 oops=0. Both legs printed
VERDICT=PASS_RING_ALLOCATION_UNDER_FRAGMENTATION with
VERDICT_SCOPE ordinary-x86_64-vzalloc-path;
co-co-chunked-fallback-not-exercised.

The lifecycle gate scores five verdicts independently: buffer
cleanup, the /dev/uioN maps, the sysfs "ring" maps, the size-class
map accounting, and device restore. The two mmap surfaces are scored
against the lifetimes they actually promise. /dev/uioN keeps its pin
until the VMA closes (hv_uio_pin_vm_ops), so its pages must still
read after teardown. The channel "ring" sysfs node is a kernfs bin
attribute with no vm_ops; hv_remove_ring_sysfs() runs before
vmbus_free_ring() and the mapping is withdrawn with the node, so a
post-teardown fault there is that ordering and not a mapping freed
under an active VMA. Both surfaces require the map to be live at
establishment and the hold to reach the release signal rather than
ending on its own.

Both legs reported CLEANUP_VERDICT=PASS, MAP_UIO_VERDICT=PASS,
MAP_SYSFS_VERDICT=PASS, MAP_ACCOUNTING_VERDICT=PASS and
RESTORE_VERDICT=PARTIAL (rebind=no), and the aggregate
LIFECYCLE_VERDICT=PARTIAL. An earlier aggregate let one successful
mapping surface stand for both; the sysfs ring specifically failed
to mmap on run 37149993984 (maps=0, EINVAL) because the path
installed .close on a kernfs vma. That is fixed in 10/13 and this
run reports maps=5 on /dev/uio0 and maps=1 on the sysfs ring on
both legs.

RESTORE_VERDICT answers one question: did hv_netvsc come back. With
rebind=no it is PARTIAL and the aggregate cannot be a complete PASS.
That is the known hv_uio_remove() gap described below, and scoring
it as PASS would be the exact over-claim the gate exists to prevent.

The end-of-run map gate scores a band, not a point. The settled
post-unbind tuple is 7 maps / 307200 bytes / 68 pages. UIO
teardown is not the inverse of UIO probe under the hv_uio_remove()
gap below, so the end must land between that tuple and the boot
baseline with the retained ring set bounded. Both legs settled at
9 / 4517888 / 1094 after two samples: a retained gap of 2 maps /
4210688 bytes / 1026 pages against a 4 / 8388608 / 2048 budget,
with PHASE 2 having released its own buffers. The owner ledger is
a size-class ledger, not an instance ledger: it reports
owner_attribution=size_class_only because /proc/vmallocinfo names
the allocating symbol and never the instance. Under that
granularity it found unidentified_retained_sizes: none on both
legs, so every retained size class is one a prior state already
accounted for. PHASE 1 returned to 12 / 20279296 / 4939 exactly on
both legs.

What is not proven here
-----------------------

CoCo page-state transitions have not been observed on confidential
hardware. The per-chunk decrypt design is what those platforms need
and the static invariants are machine-checked in CI, but that is a
negative proof, not platform evidence. CoCo hardware qualification
is set aside for this submission; the row stays PARTIAL. The
chunked degrade only runs under vmbus_needs_shared_pages(); an
ordinary x86_64 guest takes the vzalloc() path, so the runtime
numbers do not speak to the confidential fallback.

Patches 0011-0013 add no named KUnit case. The netvsc completion
path and RNDIS allocation have no targeted regression test here.

Driver rebind is not host rescind or GPADL response/reordering
interleaving. The lifecycle gate measures normal rebind; it does
not direct the hypervisor to rescind an offer or pause a create
response. That qualification remains open.

The assumption that a host rescind revokes every possible GPADL
owner is not settled by a public protocol ordering proof. Uncertain
cases are retained and accounted for rather than freed.

The allocation failure under buddy fragmentation is the measured
defect. The WSL "accept4 failed" symptom is one observed
consequence; this series does not claim every WSL symptom came
from this path.

hv_uio_remove() unregisters a UIO device without
vmbus_disconnect_ring(), so unbinding uio_hv_generic while
userspace still holds /dev/uioN leaves the channel in
CHANNEL_OPENED_STATE and the next hv_netvsc probe fails with -22.
That is pre-existing in mainline and is not fixed here. This series
does not make it worse: the ring pages stay alive while pinned
instead of being freed under an open mapping.

Device restoration is therefore not proven while the drill holds
/dev/uioN open across the unbind. That is the harder condition and
it is the one the drill measures. The verdict says so rather than
scoring a mapping as a restore.

The lifecycle drill scores five verdicts independently -- buffer
cleanup, the /dev/uioN maps, the sysfs "ring" maps, map accounting
and device restore -- and the aggregate is their conjunction: any
FAIL is a FAIL, any SKIP or PARTIAL is a PARTIAL, and only all five
PASS is a PASS. A run that establishes one mapping surface and not
the other is PARTIAL. That is deliberate: an earlier aggregate let
a single successful surface stand for both, so a sysfs ring that
never opened could be carried by a UIO map that did.

The design follows Kelley's review of v1. Comments welcome.

